        <div id="meal-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Building a 40 g Protein Pasta Bowl
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pasta alone rarely gets a meal to the 30 to 40 g of protein many people aim for at dinner. A 3 oz dry portion of Banza gives about 16 g; the same portion of Explore Cuisine edamame gives about 36 g. The smart move is to pick the pasta for taste and texture first, then let the rest of the plate fill the gap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some easy additions: a quarter cup of grated Parmesan adds roughly 8 to 10 g, 4 oz of cooked chicken breast about 30 g, a can of tuna around 20 to 25 g, and half a cup of cottage cheese blended into a sauce about 12 to 14 g. A meat sauce made with lean ground beef or turkey adds 20 g or more per portion. Any one of these on top of a chickpea or lentil pasta puts a bowl comfortably over 30 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Unflavored protein powder can go into sauces too. A scoop of unflavored whey or pea protein stirred into a cream or cheese sauce off the heat adds 20 g or more without changing much except thickness. Avoid adding it to a boiling sauce, where whey in particular can clump. Our <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">unflavored protein powder guide</a> covers which ones disappear best.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are building a full high-protein pantry, protein pasta pairs naturally with the other staples we cover, from <a href="/best-protein-pancake-mix/">protein pancake mix</a> for breakfast to <a href="/best-protein-cereal/">high-protein cereal</a> and <a href="/best-protein-chips/">protein chips</a> for snacks. None of them has to be perfect on its own; the point is that each meal contributes a little more than it used to.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> to set a daily target, then plan pasta nights backward from it. If dinner needs to deliver 40 g, a soybean pasta with Parmesan gets there alone; a chickpea pasta needs a meat, fish or dairy partner. Either works, and knowing which you need saves guessing at the stove.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing a 3.5 oz claim with a 2 oz panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Banza and Barilla print protein for a 3.5 oz portion on the front, while Explore Cuisine prints 2 oz figures. Comparing those numbers directly makes the wrong pasta look better. Always compare on the 2 oz dry Nutrition Facts serving, or multiply any 3.5 oz claim by about 0.57 first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every protein pasta is gluten-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Barilla Protein+ and the standard Goodles Cheddy Mac both contain wheat. Legume pastas are usually gluten-free, but a protein pasta is not automatically a legume pasta. If you have celiac disease or gluten sensitivity, read the ingredient list and allergen statement on every box, every time, before buying or cooking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cooking it like regular pasta</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Legume pastas go from al dente to mushy within a minute or two, and timing on the box can be generous. Use plenty of boiling water, start tasting early, and drain as soon as it has bite. Sauce it immediately, because most of these pastas stiffen as they cool.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Jumping straight to 15 g of fiber</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A serving of soybean or lupini pasta can carry four or five times the fiber of regular pasta. Going from a low-fiber diet to that in one meal is a common cause of gas and bloating. Start with a smaller portion or mix with regular pasta, drink enough water, and build up over a few weeks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the allergen on the label</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Edamame and black bean pastas are soybean, and Kaizen is lupini, a legume related to peanuts. Chickpea and lentil pastas are legumes too. If you have a soy, peanut or legume allergy, check the ingredients carefully and talk to your doctor before trying lupin. A high protein number is never worth a reaction.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which pasta has the most protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Among widely sold options, Explore Cuisine's organic black bean and edamame spaghetti lead, at about 25 g and 24 g of protein per 2 oz dry serving respectively, per labels at the time of writing. Kaizen's lupini pasta follows at about 19 to 20 g. Chickpea, lentil and wheat-blend pastas land between 10 and 14 g, compared with about 7 g for regular durum wheat pasta.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is chickpea pasta healthier than regular pasta?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Per 2 oz dry, Banza chickpea pasta has about 11 g of protein and 5 g of fiber, against roughly 7 g and 2 to 3 g for regular pasta, with slightly fewer net carbs. That makes it more filling and better for blood sugar for most people. Calories are similar, so portion size still matters. Healthier depends on your goals, but the swap is a reasonable upgrade.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is protein pasta good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can help. Higher protein and fiber make a meal more filling, which may make it easier to eat a sensible portion and stay full longer. Calories per serving are similar to regular pasta, though, so it is not a free pass. Weigh your dry portion, add vegetables and a lean protein, and use it as part of an overall calorie plan rather than a fix on its own.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Barilla Protein+ gluten-free?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  No. Barilla Protein+ is made with semolina and durum wheat blended with lentil, chickpea and pea flours, so it contains gluten. Barilla's red lentil and chickpea pastas are the brand's gluten-free options. If you need gluten-free, check for a gluten-free claim on the box and read the allergen statement, and see our gluten-free protein powder guide for supplements.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is legume protein as good as meat protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Soy protein, used in the edamame and black bean pastas, scores close to animal proteins on standard quality measures. Chickpea, lentil, pea and lupini proteins are good but lower in the sulfur amino acids. Over a day of mixed eating this rarely matters, and pairing legume pasta with cheese, meat, eggs, fish or a grain closes the gap easily.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can people with diabetes eat protein pasta?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Many people with diabetes find legume pasta easier to fit into a meal plan because of the higher fiber and lower net carbs, especially the soybean and lupini options. Responses vary between individuals, so check your own glucose after a meal and follow your care team's advice. This is general information, not medical advice, and portion size still drives the carbohydrate load.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Where should I buy protein pasta on Amazon?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buy from listings marked Ships from and sold by Amazon, or from the brand's own storefront, and compare the price per ounce rather than per pack. Multipacks and Subscribe & Save usually give the best price, but some third-party listings charge far more or ship fewer boxes than shown. Check the photographed Nutrition Facts panel before ordering.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
